> **"The D-VVS2 vs D-VVS1 question at 3ct is settled by the loupe, not the eye. I've set both grades side by side in platinum solitaires — no client has ever been able to tell them apart. What they do see is the difference between a well-cut stone and a poorly-cut one. At 3ct, cut is everything. Grade is secondary."** — Farzana Hasan, Diamond Expert

## Above $6,000 — D-VVS1 and D-IF Tier

At $6,515, the next tier is IGI D-VVS1 Very Good. At $8,257, GIA D-VVS1. At $11,108, IGI D-IF Ideal — a $4,593 jump from D-VVS1.

**The D-VVS1 trap at 3ct:** The IGI D-VVS1 at $6,515 carries a Very Good cut grade. That is a problem. At 3ct, Very Good cut means bow-tie risk. You pay $1,759 over the D-VVS2 Excellent for a clarity step that is invisible — and accept a cut grade that could be visible. Unless the 360° video confirms a clean belly, D-VVS1 Very Good at $6,515 is not better value than D-VVS2 Excellent at $4,756.

**D-IF at $11,108:** Internally Flawless means no inclusions visible at 10x to a grader. On a 3ct stone on a finger, it looks identical to VVS2. The $6,352 jump from D-VVS2 buys a certificate grade, not a better-looking stone.

